      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <div class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Life Insurance</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What happens when my 20-year term life policy expires? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            When your level term expires, coverage typically converts to an annual renewable term (ART) policy. Your premiums will jump sharply (often 500% to 1,000%) based on your attained age. Most policyholders simply let the policy expire because their financial liabilities (mortgage, raising children) have concluded and they have built retirement assets.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Are life insurance payouts taxable to my beneficiaries? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Under Internal Revenue Code Section 101(a), life insurance death benefits paid to named beneficiaries are generally 100% free of federal income tax. However, if the benefit is held by the insurer and earns interest prior to distribution, the accrued interest portion is subject to ordinary income tax.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Can I get life insurance without a medical exam? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes. Modern carriers utilize "accelerated underwriting," using electronic algorithms to check your prescription drug history (Milliman IntelliScript), motor vehicle records, and clinical lab databases (MIB). If you are reasonably healthy, carriers can approve up to $1,000,000 to $2,000,000 in term coverage in 24 to 48 hours without blood draws or physician visits.
          </div>
        </div>
